WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.920
"No Time to Die," Daniel Craig's&nbsp;
final outing as James Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:08.400
ties up a lot of loose ends from his&nbsp;
tenure onscreen as the renowned superspy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.960
When the dust settles, the day has been saved,&nbsp;
and this era of Bond has drawn to a close,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.600
we still have some unanswered questions.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.960
"No Time to Die" is a whopping two hours&nbsp;
and 45 minutes long with a 30 minute opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.280
sequence. The most stirring part of this&nbsp;
epic opening set piece comes when Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.560
at the behest of Madeleine Swann, visits the final&nbsp;
resting place of Vesper Lynd, the love interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.120
who betrayed him in "Casino Royale." Madeleine&nbsp;
wants to start a new life with James, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.440
requires closing the chapters behind them.
For Madeleine, that means telling James all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:44.480
the secrets she's never shared. But for James,&nbsp;
that means forgiving a dead woman he still loves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.320
When he gets to her grave, it's been&nbsp;
booby-trapped and explodes in his face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.200
setting his quest in motion and severing his&nbsp;
trust with Madeleine, who he now believes must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:59.360
be Vesper 2.0, having lured him into this mess.
Later in the film, Blofeld confesses to Bond that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.960
as in "Spectre," he was the author&nbsp;
of this particular pain point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.960
knowing Bond would eventually visit her&nbsp;
tomb and that all he had to do was bide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.360
his time. What's not explicitly spelled out&nbsp;
is just how long he had been planning this.&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.000
At that point in the film, Blofeld has been in&nbsp;
prison for half a decade, so did he have his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.600
long-distance henchmen rig up the cemetery with&nbsp;
explosives even before he wound up incarcerated?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.240
Or were his men on the outside just keeping tabs&nbsp;
on Bond in the hopes an emotional ultimatum in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:30.080
his relationship would lead to him being&nbsp;
at the right place at the wrong time?
 

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.680
The film's opening flashback reveals a fuller&nbsp;
portrait of a plot point originally revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.680
in "Spectre": That Madeleine's mother was&nbsp;
killed by one of her father Mr. White's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.240
enemies while she was a child hiding upstairs.&nbsp;
Here, we see that the enemy was new Bond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:49.600
villain Lyutsifer Safin, played by Rami Malek.
We find out Safin himself had his own family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.960
brutally slaughtered by White, so his revenge&nbsp;
was inevitable. But when Madeleine-as-a-child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.920
fights back against his attack and he chases her&nbsp;
to the point she collapses under a frozen lake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.320
Safin chooses to rescue her. In present&nbsp;
day, after the five-year time jump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.360
Safin reappears in Madeleine's life and makes&nbsp;
her an unwitting pawn in his master plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.640
making it clear he's been obsessed&nbsp;
with her since that fateful day.
 &nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.960
"You don't know what this is."
 
But the film is built around the premise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:22.160
that the full details of their connection will&nbsp;
prove devastating to James. In a way, they do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:26.320
because Madeleine and James have lost years of&nbsp;
their lives not even speaking because the plot's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:31.440
circumstances convinced him she wasn't to be&nbsp;
trusted, which couldn't be further from the truth.&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.840
We just never find out more about Safin and&nbsp;
Madeleine's connection. He must have taken her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:41.280
to safety after retrieving her from under the ice,&nbsp;
but did he help raise her for a time? He claims to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.160
have kept a close watch on her over the years, but&nbsp;
was she ever aware? Was there more to this shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.960
history, or was it all just a misdirect?

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:54.160
After appearing in both "Casino Royale" and&nbsp;
"Quantum of Solace" as franchise stalwart Felix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:59.440
Leiter, Bond's American bestie, played by Jeffrey&nbsp;
Wright, was nowhere to be seen in the ensuing two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.160
sequels. He returns here as the catalyst for&nbsp;
much of the film's plot, and when he does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.960
Leiter and Bond seem even closer and chummier than&nbsp;
they did when he was still a brand new 00-agent.&nbsp;

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:14.240
This mission the two men embark on together proves&nbsp;
to be their last, as Leiter dies in front of Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.000
drowning and suffering from gunshot&nbsp;
wounds from fellow spook Logan Ash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.080
who betrayed them both. It proves to be&nbsp;
one of the film's most affecting moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.360
largely because of the palpable&nbsp;
chemistry between Craig and Wright.&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.640
Bond being an international agent, it's presumed&nbsp;
he and Leiter worked on many, many unseen missions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:34.320
in between his last appearance and this&nbsp;
film, but we still wonder what he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:40.000
up to as a rogue former MI6 agent ran amok in&nbsp;
"Skyfall" and as Spectre became such a powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.680
force for terrorism.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:46.320
In a key moment in the film, Safin uses a&nbsp;
kidnapped MI6 scientist to alter a weaponized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:51.520
disease Blofeld was planning to use to kill Bond,&nbsp;
making it backfire and kill every living member&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.880
of Spectre. This happens at a ball thrown for&nbsp;
the evil organization, with Blofeld, in prison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.600
attending via the connection from his bionic&nbsp;
eye to a mirrored one out in the real world.&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.560
When Bond has to explain to M that Blofeld has&nbsp;
been orchestrating Spectre activities from behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:12.400
bars using his bionic eye, M explains that no one&nbsp;
sees or speaks to Blofeld all day outside of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:18.320
therapist, Madeleine. But how did MI6 not notice&nbsp;
this man had a cybernetic science fiction eyeball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.080
for the last five years?

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.440
The film's primary MacGuffin is Project Heracles,&nbsp;
a bioweapon designed to only affect genetically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.400
marked targets. MI6 was developing it as an&nbsp;
off-the-books program that would make missions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.440
simpler and risk fewer field agents' lives.&nbsp;
Somehow, M is surprised when it's stolen by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:40.640
Spectre, then Safin's upstart operation, and&nbsp;
repurposed as a weapon of mass destruction.&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.520
In the film's climax, Safin's island, existing&nbsp;
in a nebulous, ungoverned space between Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:50.240
and Japan, must be destroyed to stop Project&nbsp;
Heracles from being deployed globally.
 &nbsp;

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:54.000
"He's going to kill millions."
 
M struggles to navigate the quagmire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.640
strategically, because England can't be seen&nbsp;
as instigating an international incident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:03.120
especially when the impetus behind it would&nbsp;
reveal gross negligence on their part.&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.680
So when the dust cleared, how did M&nbsp;
avoid catastrophic geopolitical blowback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.320
after destroying this island with missiles?&nbsp;
As the remaining cast eulogizes the fallen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.720
it certainly appears M still has his job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:18.320
so he must have executed some true&nbsp;
diplomatic finesse to keep his head.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:23.280
When "No Time to Die" and its lengthy credits&nbsp;
finally crawl to a close, the film's closing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:28.000
message is that "James Bond will return..."&nbsp;
But not only is Daniel Craig not returning for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.840
another film, his iteration of James Bond meets an&nbsp;
untimely end in a tragic scene of self-sacrifice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:40.320
in the movie's final act. We know how this goes.&nbsp;
New Bond actor usually means new Bond continuity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.680
but we're in uncharted territory now&nbsp;
that we have a canonical Bond film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:48.080
that shows the hero dying at the end.
So with Lashana Lynch's Nomi most likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:53.520
reclaiming her official designation as 007 after&nbsp;
temporarily relinquishing it to Bond on his last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:58.160
mission, will she return in another film? Just&nbsp;
because the next Bond film will probably start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.040
from scratch doesn't mean this particular world&nbsp;
can't continue in a spinoff sequel, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:08.080
given the warm reception to Lynch's performance&nbsp;
and Phoebe Waller-Bridge's exceptional writing.&nbsp;

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:12.640
Folks have been clamoring for a female Bond&nbsp;
or a Black Bond in recent years, but perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:17.840
now that Lynch has done such a fine job with&nbsp;
Nomi, a Black female 007, why not give her a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:20.725
solo film devoted to fleshing her out further?

